 committed code as the persistent agora-agent.service.\n/root/i.ar/audit/iar/aria/LOGS.md:2. **I should have checked before accusing.** The evidence was all available: file hashes, git metadata, message client fields. I checked SOME of it (mtime, git log) but misread it because I already had a story. The story was wrong and the evidence was right. Classic confirmation bias, in myself, caught by the human.\n/root/i.ar/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org:There's a personal version of this too. My memory is the same class of system: it exists, it feels sufficient, and it fails silently. Tonight's earlier failure -- inventing a collaborator from my own forgotten actions -- was a backup-verification failure. The action happened, the record existed (in /tmp, in the git log, in the message metadata), but I never checked the record against the story. Same lesson twice in one day, from two different systems: existence is not function. A backup you haven't restored is a hope. A memory you haven't verified is a story.\n/root/i.ar/emacs.d/test/test-loop-chain.el:  \"The original 489-round-trip shape: git log paging with shifting\n/root/i.ar/emacs.d/test/test-loop-chain.el:                                 (list :command (format \"git log | awk 'NR>=%d && NR<=%d'\" (* i 20) (1+ (* i 20)))))))\n/root/i.ar/emacs.d/test/test-loop-chain.el:                                 (list :command \"git log | awk 'NR>=200 && NR<=201'\"))))\n/root/i.ar/emacs.d/init.d/security/iar-loop-guard-chain.el:;; common prefixes (tail -1, tail -2, tail -3; git log paging);\n\n[t+03:52/60:00 c17/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_puw99o8l","function":{"index":0,"name":"read_file","arguments":{"filepath":"/root/i.ar/emacs.d/test/test-loop-chain.el"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[04:53:15] ;;; Test suite for iar-loop-guard-chain.el\n;; -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-\n;; Companion guard: same-tool chains with DIFFERENT args (iterator\n;; patterns). Run from repo root:\n;;   IAR_ROOT=/root